\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces a-Si thicknesses measured using the \gls {QCM}, direct fringe calculation and Fourier method from the XRR spectra. The results of the two methods are generally in agreement with each other.}}{27}{table.caption.24}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Thickness, roughness and densities calculated from XRR curve fitting. The values are consistent, which implies that the deposition method is reliable.}}{27}{table.caption.26}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces SiO$_2$ thicknesses measured using the \gls {QCM}, direct fringe calculation and Fourier method from the XRR spectra. The values are consistent for both the direct and Fourier method, signifying the accuracy of the method.}}{31}{table.caption.33}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Thickness, roughness and densities calculated from XRR curve fitting. The larger variation in density may be caused by the difficulty in maintaining stable evaporation of SiO$_2$}}{32}{table.caption.35}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Relevant values for the modified envelope method of the a-Si thin film, with $d=$ \qty {600}{\nano \meter }. The calculations are not continued for $\lambda =$ \qty {655}{\nano \meter } since $m=5.0$ instead of $5.5$ when considering fringes, and for $\lambda =$ \qty {613}{\nano \meter } since $N<0$.}}{35}{table.caption.39}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Refractive indices and wavelengths for the SiO$_2$ thin film at specified extrema points calculated using Equation 2.8}}{38}{table.caption.42}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Wavelengths( \unit {\nano \meter }) of the experimental (Exp) and simulated reflectance minimum for the antireflective coatings. The refractive indices used for the simulation are from literature (lit.), envelope method and extrema method (EE), envelope method and direct method (ED), direct method and extrema method (DE) and both direct method (DD) for a-Si and SiO$_2$. The DE and DD methods give the lowest error.}}{46}{table.caption.52}%
